\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 The Digest\b0\par\par\uc2 By Katie Robertson and The Associated Press \par \uc2 559 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 April 2026\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 MEDIA\par \par \uc2 Court Dismisses Lawsuit By Fox News Employee\par \par \uc2 A lawsuit brought against Fox News by a reporter who said the network fired him after he spoke out against its coverage of the 2020 presidential election and the Jan. 6 Capitol riot was dismissed on Monday by a federal judge.\par \par \uc2 Jason Donner, who was a reporter and producer at Fox News for 12 years, said in his lawsuit that he had repeatedly raised concerns to his bosses about Fox's amplification of false claims around the election and the 2021 attack on the U.S. Capitol. He said he was fired in September 2022 after calling in sick for the day, and claimed retaliation and workplace discrimination.\par \par \uc2 The judge, Amir H. Ali of U.S. District Court for the District of Columbia, granted summary judgment on Monday in favor of Fox on Mr. Donner's claim of retaliation under the District of Columbia's Accrued Sick and Safe Leave Act. Judge Ali found that Mr. Donner had not notified his manager as early as possible that he was sick and that Fox had not unlawfully fired Mr. Donner for taking a sick day.\par \par \uc2 Another judge, Judge Christopher R. Cooper, dismissed the other claims in the lawsuit in April 2024, five months after it was filed, saying Mr. Donner failed to prove he had been discriminated and retaliated against because of his ''political affiliation'' for not aligning himself with President Trump and did not show grounds for his wrongful-termination claim.\par \par \uc2 ''When it comes to employment law in the District of Columbia, the allegation that a journalist was terminated for insisting that his network report the truth and hold wrongdoers accountable does not amount to an actionable claim,'' Judge Cooper wrote then.\par \par \uc2 KATIE ROBERTSON\par \par \uc2 ECONOMY\par \par \uc2 Consumer Confidence\par \par \uc2 Rises Despite Gas Prices\par \par \uc2 U.S. consumer confidence inched higher in March despite soaring energy prices brought on by the war in Iran.\par \par \uc2 The Conference Board said Tuesday that its consumer confidence index rose modestly to 91.8 in March from 91 in February.\par \par \uc2 The board said that while rising costs due to tariffs and spiking oil prices induced by the Middle East war did not affect the topline confidence reading, there was increasing pessimism in other measures of the survey, including expectations of higher inflation.\par \par \uc2 Respondents' comments about oil, gas and the war increased and consumers' 12-month inflation expectations surged to levels last seen in August 2025 when anxiety over tariffs peaked.\par \par \uc2 U.S. gas prices jumped past an average of $4 a gallon for the first time since 2022 on Tuesday as the war caused fuel prices to soar worldwide.\par \par \uc2 According to AAA, the national average for a gallon of regular gasoline is now $4.02, up more than a dollar before the war began. The last time U.S. drivers were paying this much at the pump was nearly four years ago, after Russia's invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 ''This is the key concern as the war in Iran enters the second month,'' wrote Heather Long, chief economist at \uc2 Navy Federal Credit Union\uc2 . ''Will the oil price shock turn into a demand destruction shock?''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Long said credit card data from March showed that consumers were still making purchases across categories even as gas prices rose. Associated Press\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020260401em410002x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
