\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Talks Go On For Senators, Wait Goes On For Travelers\b0\par\par\uc2 By Michael Gold and Megan Mineiro \par \uc2 1,231 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 March 2026\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 President Trump's announcement came as talks on Capitol Hill over funding the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  faltered, and airport lines continued to grow.\par \par \uc2 President Trump said on Thursday that he would sign an emergency order to pay \uc2 Transportation Security Administration\uc2  agents who have gone without compensation for weeks, as senators struggled to strike a homeland security funding deal that could end the intensifying crisis at airports.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump did not provide any details, but the administration was expected to use funds provided to the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  last year as part of his tax cut and domestic policy law, according to a senior administration official and another person familiar with the plan, both of whom spoke on the condition of anonymity to describe it.\par \par \uc2 No executive order, emergency or otherwise, would be required to access those funds, which were enacted into law last summer, but Mr. Trump appeared eager to claim credit for releasing them. It was not clear why he had waited more than five weeks after the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  was shuttered to direct that they be used to pay T.S.A. employees.\par \par \uc2 Travelers are facing long waits at airport security checkpoints, in some cases more than two hours, as the partial shutdown continues. About 50,000 T.S.A. personnel have been working without pay for over a month, and hundreds have quit or stopped appearing for work.\par \par \uc2 As lawmakers have struggled to come up with a deal to fund the department, Mr. Trump and Republicans in Congress have repeatedly blamed Democrats for depriving T.S.A. agents of their pay. Democrats had been pressing to separately fund T.S.A. for weeks, an approach that Republicans rejected time and time again after \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  funding lapsed on Feb. 14.\par \par \uc2 But until Thursday, neither side had wanted to budge on money for T.S.A. -- the part of the department that most visibly affects Americans' daily lives -- as each sought leverage in a broader dispute over Mr. Trump's immigration crackdown. That fight began last month when, following the killings of two U.S. citizens by federal immigration agents, Democrats refused to fund the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  without measures to rein in enforcement tactics.\par \par \uc2 With long lines at airports ahead of a prime spring travel weekend, and with lawmakers eager to compromise ahead of a two-week congressional recess, Senate Democrats and Republicans had stepped up their efforts in recent days to reach a funding deal, and spent Thursday haggling over the terms.\par \par \uc2 Yet as the pace of conversations picked up on Capitol Hill, there was still no sign of a compromise by evening, which Mr. Trump acknowledged in a social media post announcing his plan.\par \par \uc2 ''I am going to sign an Order instructing the Secretary of Homeland Security, \uc2 Markwayne Mullin\uc2 , to immediately pay our TSA Agents in order to address this Emergency Situation,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 He lashed out at Democrats, who he said had ''recklessly created a true National Crisis'' by refusing to fund the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2 . And he said he had been moved to intervene personally.\par \par \uc2 ''It is not an easy thing to do,'' Mr. Trump said, ''but I am going to do it!''\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 John Barrasso\uc2  of Wyoming, the No. 2 Republican, acknowledged that senators, who have traditionally fought to preserve their power of the purse, ''would have preferred to get this done here on the floor of the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 John Thune\uc2 , Republican of South Dakota and the majority leader, called Mr. Trump's announcement a ''short-term solution,'' suggesting that Republicans would keep pressing to reach a broader deal.\par \par \uc2 ''Obviously we want to fund everything within'' the \uc2 Homeland Security Department\uc2 , he said.\par \par \uc2 Democrats slammed Mr. Trump's intervention, saying they would continue pressing for immigration enforcement changes. Representative Rosa DeLauro of Connecticut, the top Democrat on the Appropriations Committee, said that lawmakers still needed to negotiate a funding bill.\par \par \uc2 ''I am glad that this administration has finally chosen to pay these workers, after choosing not to for 41 days,'' she said in a statement. ''The administration must provide an explanation as to what funding it is using to pay these workers after falsely claiming it could not do so.''\par \par \uc2 Republicans on Thursday morning sent Democrats what Mr. Thune called ''our last and final'' offer. It would have funded the department without money for \uc2 Immigration and Customs Enforcement\uc2 's deportation and detention operations.\par \par \uc2 But Democrats have continued to insist that any spending measure still include limits on federal immigration agents, and contain guardrails to prevent funds from be moved from other parts of the department to be used for immigration enforcement efforts.\par \par \uc2 Senator Chris Coons, Democrat of Delaware, hinted that the talks were making headway, saying that the ''back-and-forth'' was continuing between the two parties.\par \par \uc2 ''I think the good news is that there is very broad agreement that we have to fund T.S.A.,'' he said. ''The bad news is that there's not yet agreement on exactly how to fund D.H.S. without Democrats funding ICE, and we're trying to get clarity on exactly what that looks like.''\par \par \uc2 The negotiating was happening in closed-door meetings and in public, as bipartisan groups of senators huddled in the Senate chamber to discuss the shape of a potential deal. Still, by Thursday evening, optimism had faded.\par \par \uc2 Senator Mike Rounds, Republican of South Dakota, looked glum when he was asked whether lawmakers had made progress on negotiations.\par \par \uc2 ''No,'' he said, shaking his head before heading to the Senate floor.\par \par \uc2 Earlier Thursday, the House separately voted to pass a bill to fully fund the Homeland Security Department, the third time in two months that the chamber had done so. Four Democrats joined Republicans in supporting the bill, which passed 218 to 206.\par \par \uc2 Still, a funding bill remains stalled in the Senate, where 60 votes are required for major legislation to move forward.\par \par \uc2 Senator John Kennedy, Republican of Louisiana, had said he would push for a separate bill that would fund just the T.S.A. And before Mr. Trump's announcement, officials had been weighing whether they could find a way to pay T.S.A. agents by using funds that Republicans gave the department last year as part of their sweeping tax and domestic policy bill.\par \par \uc2 That measure handed a slush fund to ICE, and billions more to the rest of the Homeland Security Department with few limitations, including $10 billion for ''border support.''\par \par \uc2 Senator Susan Collins, Republican of Maine and the chairwoman of the Appropriations Committee, told reporters on Thursday that the Office of Management and Budget had determined that Mr. Trump had the authority to use the funds in the law to pay T.S.A. workers.\par \par \uc2 Everett Kelley, the president of the union representing T.S.A. employees, said in a statement that the union's ''call was answered'' with Mr. Trump's announcement and that members were ''grateful that action was taken.'' Mr. Kelley also called on lawmakers to fund the rest of D.H.S. ''These workers and their families cannot wait,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Carl Hulse, Tony Romm, Chris Cameron, Gabe Castro-Root contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Carl Hulse, Tony Romm, Chris Cameron, Gabe Castro-Root contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020260327em3r00049\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
