\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Meta\uc2  Lays Off 700 Employees, While Rewarding Top Executives\b0\par\par\uc2 By Eli Tan \par \uc2 710 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 March 2026\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2  on Wednesday laid off around 700 employees, a person with knowledge of the company said, the latest downsizing as the Silicon Valley giant shifts its priorities toward artificial intelligence.\par \par \uc2 Less than 24 hours earlier, the company unveiled a new stock program for six top executives that could increase compensation for some of them by as much as $921 million each over the next five years. \uc2 Meta\uc2  said the move was a way to retain talent in the A.I. era and push it toward ambitious growth.\par \par \uc2 The dichotomy -- cutting some employees while rewarding high-ranking executives -- underlines how much A.I. has changed the tech industry. In recent years, \uc2 Meta\uc2  has been trying to move beyond its social media and metaverse businesses. \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2  , \uc2 Meta\uc2  's chief executive, has declared that he is striving to create ''superintelligence,'' or a godlike A.I. that can act as the ultimate personal companion.\par \par \uc2 Last year, Mr. Zuckerberg shelled out billions of dollars to hire a team of A.I. specialists. At the same time, the company planned to cut 10 percent to 15 percent of Reality Labs, its division making virtual reality and metaverse products.\par \par \uc2 The latest layoffs compounded a tough day for \uc2 Meta\uc2  , which owns \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Instagram\uc2  and \uc2 WhatsApp\uc2  . A Los Angeles jury on Wednesday found the company liable for harming a young user with addictive design features on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  in a bellwether case that could open social media companies to more lawsuits over users' well-being.\par \par \uc2 While the verdict was coming in, \uc2 Meta\uc2  was also announcing the 700 layoffs in the Reality Labs unit, as well as some in recruiting, sales and \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , a person with knowledge of the matter said. The layoffs were a fraction of the tech giant's 78,000 employees, but signal \uc2 Meta\uc2  's priorities.\par \par \uc2 ''Teams across \uc2 Meta\uc2  regularly restructure or implement changes to ensure they're in the best position to achieve their goals,'' a \uc2 Meta\uc2  spokesman said in a statement. ''Where possible, we are finding other opportunities for employees whose positions may be impacted.''\par \par \uc2 The layoffs were earlier reported by The Information.\par \par \uc2 Before the job cuts, \uc2 Meta\uc2  shared details of its new stock program for six top executives. They were \uc2 Andrew Bosworth\uc2  , the chief technology officer; \uc2 Chris Cox\uc2  , the chief product officer; \uc2 Susan Li\uc2  , its chief financial officer; \uc2 Javier Olivan\uc2  , the chief operating officer; \uc2 Dina Powell McCormick\uc2  , the president and vice chairman; and C.J. Mahoney, the company's chief legal officer.\par \par \uc2 The program allows the executives to buy additional \uc2 Meta\uc2  stock options if the company hits certain growth targets. The most aggressive target is for \uc2 Meta\uc2  to become a $9 trillion company by 2031. Its current market capitalization stands at around $1.5 trillion.\par \par \uc2 If it achieves the goals, the new stock options for some of the executives -- such as Mr. Bosworth, Mr. Cox and Mr. Olivan -- would be worth as much as $921 million each, according to an analysis by \uc2 Equilar\uc2  , a compensation research firm. Ms. Li's stock options would be worth as much as $161 million, the firm said.\par \par \uc2 It was the first time \uc2 Meta\uc2  gave executives stock options since it went public in 2012, when the company was named \uc2 Facebook\uc2  . In a statement, \uc2 Meta\uc2  said the program was meant to keep the company competitive with A.I. rivals and incentivize executives. Mr. Zuckerberg was not given new stock options.\par \par \uc2 ''This is a big bet,'' a \uc2 Meta\uc2  spokesman said in a statement. ''These pay packages will not be realized unless \uc2 Meta\uc2  achieves massive future success, benefiting all of our shareholders.''\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2  has forecast that it will spend at least $115 billion this year, primarily on A.I., including on the construction of new data centers to power the technology. Mr. Zuckerberg has also said that A.I. will change how employees work, with A.I. tools allowing fewer employees to get more work done.\par \par \uc2 ''I think 2026 is going to be the year that A.I. starts to dramatically change the way that we work,'' he said on a call with investors in January. ''We're starting to see projects that used to require big teams now be accomplished by a single very talented person.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020260326em3q0003w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
