\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Changing the Lesson Plan on Chavez, Many Teachers Focus on the Movement\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jesus Jim\u233\'00\'E9nez \par \uc2 1,214 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 March 2026\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 21\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 In classrooms across the country, educators are weighing whether to shift focus from Chavez to the broader labor movement he helped lead.\par \par \uc2 Miguel Sandoval's ninth graders in Los Angeles were learning about the Delano, Calif., grape strike, organized by agricultural workers in the 1960s, when a student asked about Cesar Chavez, a major figure in the yearslong labor action.\par \par \uc2 ''A student asked me, in front of all the students, 'Hey, did you hear about Cesar Chavez?''' Mr. Sandoval said.\par \par \uc2 The student was referring to a \uc2 New York Times\uc2  investigation published March 18 that revealed that Mr. Chavez had sexually abused two young girls and also raped Dolores Huerta, who co-founded the \uc2 United Farm Workers\uc2  union with him.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Sandoval had read the Times investigation the morning it appeared, but he said that he had to pause and think about how to answer his student.\par \par \uc2 ''Do I say the thing, the truth, in a room full of ninth graders?'' Mr. Sandoval said. The answer was yes, ''because it's just true.''\par \par \uc2 In the days since the article about Mr. Chavez, The Times connected with educators and school administrators across the country who are reckoning with how they will now teach the history of Mr. Chavez, the allegations against him and the Latino civil rights movement as a whole.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Sandoval, who teaches high-school ethnic studies, said that he had not settled on long-term adjustments to his curriculum. After the Times article emerged, he said, he had received guidance from his district to focus on the 1960s labor movement as a whole, and not just on an individual.\par \par \uc2 ''Heroes are elevated, and then the everyday people, the background player, or even the women who are the backbone of the movement, aren't always talked about,'' Mr. Sandoval said. ''There's something there for ethnic studies and history teachers to think about, which is: How do we move away from the sort of purification of individuals?''\par \par \uc2 The Times investigation on Mr. Chavez drew on interviews, internal records and firsthand accounts to document years of abuse of girls in the movement. It also included a new allegation from Ms. Huerta, who said Mr. Chavez assaulted her decades ago. Ms. Huerta, now 95, said she had kept the accusation private, fearing its impact.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, Ms. Huerta said Mr. Chavez's actions had not reflected the values of the movement and she expressed deep sorrow for others who she believes had suffered in silence for years.\par \par \uc2 For some educators, also, the news about Mr. Chavez was personal.\par \par \uc2 Diego Frias, a Mexican American social studies teacher in the Houston area, said Mr. Chavez inspired him and some of his students, many of whom are Latino or Black.\par \par \uc2 ''I was disappointed, hurt and angry at what Chavez did with the power and trust he was given by his community,'' Mr. Frias said.\par \par \uc2 Like many teachers, Mr. Frias said he plans to focus on ''the movement, not the man.''\par \par \uc2 ''This new understanding will hopefully help my students to see that they should never put their trust blindly in anyone, no matter their cause or charisma,'' Mr. Frias said. ''If my students come to understand that, the next generation of civil rights leaders will be all the better for it.''\par \par \uc2 In Texas, Aaron Kinsey, the chairman of the State Board of Education and a Republican, said he is seeking Mr. Chavez's removal from the state's public school curriculum. The board sets the standard for curriculum and instruction materials.\par \par \uc2 In a letter to the education commissioner, Mr. Kinsey called the revelations about Mr. Chavez ''horrific'' and said the civil rights leader should no longer be included in required lesson plans.\par \par \uc2 ''Americans across the political spectrum are disgusted by the facts that have come to light about the sexual abuse committed by Cesar Chavez,'' Mr. Kinsey wrote in a letter first reported by The Texas Bullpen.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, the \uc2 Texas Education Agency\uc2 , which is led by the education commissioner and implements state policy, instructed school systems in the state to eliminate or change activities and lessons related to Mr. Chavez.\par \par \uc2 Diego Bernal, a Texas state representative and Democrat who is vice chair of the legislature's public education committee, said that the news about Mr. Chavez should prompt conversations about how to teach him in school.\par \par \uc2 ''You can't leave things as they are, right?'' said Mr. Bernal, a Democrat. ''There's erasure, which is on one end, and the other is full disclosure, full context.''\par \par \uc2 Karma Chavez, a professor in the Mexican American and Latina/o Studies department at the \uc2 University of Texas at Austin\uc2 , said The Times story was sobering. As chairwoman of the department, she said her department shies ''from great man perspective on history,'' because movements are not built on one individual.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Ch\u225\'00\'E1vez said she anticipates having students read The Times story and Ms. Huerta's response, and to discuss hero worship, sexism and how movements can ''be more ethical.''\par \par \uc2 The reckoning is not limited to high schools and colleges. Elise DeFazio, who teaches sixth grade in the San Francisco Bay Area, said that for some age groups, topics such as sexual abuse are ''heavy.''\par \par \uc2 ''When it's something that you have to teach and you kind of can't get around, I think you just have to address it,'' Ms. DeFazio said. ''And you have to acknowledge it, and that conversation definitely looks different at different age groups.''\par \par \uc2 Patricia Orozco Palagi, a high school teacher in Southern California, had started to shift away from educating her classes about Mr. Chavez years ago.\par \par \uc2 After watching a documentary on Ms. Huerta a few years ago, Ms. Orozco Palagi said, she had felt a ''little embarrassed'' to have downplayed her contributions to securing better wages and conditions for farm workers. Ms. Orozco Palagi said that she had grown uncomfortable with Mr. Chavez's leadership, his treatment of Ms. Huerta and how he interacted with Filipinos, who played a crucial role in the Delano grape strike.\par \par \uc2 ''We have a tendency in history for 'great man' history, and it's so much more complex than that,'' Ms. Orozco Palagi. ''And at least as a history teacher, I want my students to think critically about everybody and everything, including Cesar Chavez.''\par \par \uc2 Still, processing the news about Mr. Chavez has not been easy, Ms. Orozco Palagi said.\par \par \uc2 ''We have so few heroes that it is really painful for us to see someone exposed like this,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Over the past week, some elected officials have asked for more lessons focused on Ms. Huerta. But Ms. Orozco Palagi, Mr. Sandoval and others feel that simply removing Mr. Chavez from street signs and textbooks is reductive.\par \par \uc2 ''We need to acknowledge the reality, and what happened, and include the voices of the people that were hurt by it, and make that a part of the story,'' Mr. Sandoval said. ''It has to be a part of the story where we acknowledge that this happened -- not just a removal.\par \par \uc2 ''Keep him in the story, but then tell the truth,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Lauren McGaughy and Jennifer Medina contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Lauren McGaughy and Jennifer Medina contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020260325em3p0004k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
