\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 The Digest\b0\par\par\uc2 By Associated Press and John Carreyrou \par \uc2 505 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 March 2026\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 AUTOMOTIVE\par \par \uc2 Battery Maker Lays Off Nearly 1,000 Workers\par \par \uc2 SK Battery America Inc.\uc2  laid off nearly 1,000 workers at a manufacturing plant northeast of Atlanta on Friday amid automakers' changing electrification plans and uncertain consumer demand for EVs.\par \par \uc2 The company said Friday marked the last working day for 958 plant employees, about 37 percent of its workforce. Affected workers will be paid through May 6. The plant will continue to employ about 1,600 workers.\par \par \uc2 SK opened the $2.6 billion battery plant in Commerce, Ga., in January 2022. The Korean company notably supplied the Ford F-150 Lightning electric pickup truck.\par \par \uc2 Ford announced plans to cancel the fully electric version of the truck in December.\par \par \uc2 The news comes as the U.S. electric vehicle market is at a standstill amid the Trump administration steering federal support away from it in favor of more lax automotive emissions policies and a broader agenda supporting the oil and gas industries.\par \par \uc2 SK Americas spokesperson Joe Guy Collier said in a statement that the workforce reduction was made to align operations to market conditions.\par \par \uc2 ''SK Battery America remains committed to Georgia and to building a robust U.S. supply chain for advanced battery manufacturing,'' Mr. Collier said. ''We are pursuing a range of future customers, including the Battery Electric Storage System arena.''\par \par \uc2 The City of Commerce and the Jackson County commission chair did not immediately respond to requests for comment on Friday. Associated Press\par \par \uc2 HEALTH CARE\par \par \uc2 Ex-Executive Is Charged\par \par \uc2 With Covering Up Deaths\par \par \uc2 Federal prosecutors charged a former executive of ExThera Medical, a California medical-device manufacturer, with concealing from the government the deaths of two cancer patients who were treated with the company's blood filter.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Sanja Ilic, ExThera's former chief regulatory officer, agreed to plead guilty and faces up to three years in prison, the Justice Department said. Prosecutors also entered into a deferred prosecution agreement with ExThera in which the company admitted that it defrauded and misled the Food and Drug Administration by failing to report the deaths.\par \par \uc2 The company agreed to pay a criminal penalty of $750,000, which will be earmarked for families of the victims, and to forfeit $5.7 million.\par \par \uc2 The two patients, David Hudlow of Panama City, Fla., and Kyle Chupp of Orillia, Ontario, died one day apart in April 2024, shortly after returning home from having their blood filtered on the Caribbean island of Antigua.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Hudlow, 55, and Mr. Chupp, 39, were among two dozen patients with late-stage cancer diagnoses whom ExThera lured to Antigua with promises that its blood filter, which the F.D.A. had conditionally approved only for the treatment of Covid-19, could cure them, \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  reported last year. Patients were charged $45,000 per treatment session.\par \par \uc2 The Antigua operation was the brainchild of Alan Quasha, an American billionaire who saw promise in ExThera's blood filter as a cancer and longevity treatment. John Carreyrou\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020260307em370004p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
