\par\par \uc2 STOCKS &AMP; BONDS\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Market Drops With a Weaker Jobs Report as Oil Surges\b0\par\par\uc2 By The Associated Press \par \uc2 696 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 March 2026\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Oil shot to its highest price since 2023 after surging again Friday because of the U.S.-Israeli war against Iran, and a weak update on the U.S. job market knocked stocks lower to cap Wall Street's worst week since October.\par \par \uc2 The S&P 500 dropped 1.3 percent after a report showed U.S. employers cut more jobs last month than they created and after oil prices spiked above $90 per barrel. The combination of a weak economy and high inflation is a worst-case scenario for investors because the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  has no good tool to fix both problems at the same time.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones industrial average plunged as many as 945 points before finishing with a loss of 453, or 0.9 percent, and the Nasdaq composite sank 1.6 percent.\par \par \uc2 Stagflation is what economists call the miserable mix of a stagnating economy with high inflation, and a separate report released Friday added to the sourness after showing that U.S. retailers made less money in January than economists expected. It raised the disconcerting possibility that spending by U.S. households, the main engine of the economy, may be stretched near its maximum.\par \par \uc2 Usually when the economy is unsteady and the job market is weakening, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  cuts interest rates to give things a boost. Lower rates can make it easier for households to get mortgages and for companies to raise money to expand, while also lifting prices for stocks and other investments. The Fed cut its main interest rate several times last year and had indicated more were to come this year.\par \par \uc2 But lower interest rates can also make inflation worse. And the Fed's hands may be increasingly tied because spiking oil prices are pushing inflation higher due to disruptions for the energy industry.\par \par \uc2 The price for a barrel of Brent crude, the international standard, leaped another 8.5 percent to settle at $92.69. It briefly rose above $94 to touch its highest level since September 2023. A barrel of benchmark U.S. crude breached the $90 level for the first time since 2023 and jumped 12.2 percent to $90.90.\par \par \uc2 If oil prices spike further, like to $100 per barrel, and stay there, some analysts and investors say it could be too much for the global economy to withstand.\par \par \uc2 To be sure, the U.S. stock market has a history of bouncing back relatively quickly following conflicts in the Middle East and elsewhere, as long as oil prices don't jump too high for too long. Uncertainty about just how high oil prices will go this time around and for how long caused frenetic swings across financial markets this past week, sometimes hour by hour.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, the S&P 500 tumbled to an immediate 1.2 percent loss at the start of trading but made it all back and ended the day with a tiny gain.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump's most recent signal on the war was that he wants an ''unconditional surrender'' of Iran, apparently ruling out negotiations.\par \par \uc2 In the bond market, Treasury yields wavered, with higher oil prices pushing upward on them and the discouraging updates on the U.S. economy pulling downward. The yield on the 10-year Treasury initially rose toward 4.19 percent before pulling back to 4.15 percent. That's up from 4.13 percent late Thursday and just 3.97 percent a week earlier.\par \par \uc2 Smaller companies often feel the bite of high borrowing costs more because many need to borrow to grow.\par \par \uc2 The Russell 2000 index of small stocks fell a market-leading 2.3 percent.\par \par \uc2 Among the big companies in the S&P 500, those with high fuel bills helped lead the way lower. \uc2 Old Dominion Freight Line\uc2  sank 7.9 percent, cruise line Carnival fell 5 percent and \uc2 Southwest Airlines\uc2  lost 5.3 percent.\par \par \uc2 In stock markets abroad, indexes slumped in Europe following a better finish in Asia. London's FTSE 100 fell 1.2 percent, while Hong Kong's Hang Seng jumped 1.7 percent.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020260307em370004l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
