\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 February Job Losses Signal Trouble for Economy\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sydney Ember \par \uc2 1,195 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 March 2026\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Employers shed 92,000 jobs in February and the unemployment rate rose to 4.4 percent.\par \par \uc2 Hiring fizzled in February, a sign of unexpected weakness in the labor market that sent warning signs flashing through the broader economy.\par \par \uc2 Employers slashed 92,000 jobs last month, the Labor Department reported on Friday, with losses cutting across nearly all major sectors. The unemployment rate ticked up to 4.4 percent.\par \par \uc2 The report dimmed the picture of the labor market, injecting a surprising note of caution into an economy already reeling from chaos in energy markets brought on by the war in Iran and fresh unknowns over trade policy. And it all but foreclosed the prospect of a swift resurgence in job growth after an anemic year of hiring that was weighed down by economic uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 ''This is a rough report,'' said Cory Stahle, an economist at the Indeed Hiring Lab. ''You might even say this is a bad report.''\par \par \uc2 Many economists had forecast that employers would shake off their reluctance to hire this year, encouraged by surprisingly robust job growth in January. The latest figures signaled that renewed vigor in the economy could take longer to materialize than anticipated.\par \par \uc2 Revisions to previous months bolstered the case that the job cuts in February were consistent with a broader decline rather than a blip. In December, employers shed 17,000 jobs, down from an earlier estimate of a gain of 48,000, and hiring figures for January were also revised downward slightly, to 126,000. Taken together, job growth for the last three months effectively slowed to zero.\par \par \uc2 Employment in February fell in industries across the board, including in manufacturing, which lost 12,000 jobs. Leisure and hospitality shed 27,000 jobs. Construction cut 11,000 jobs.\par \par \uc2 The most destabilizing losses occurred in health care and social assistance, whose growth had for months helped offset sluggishness elsewhere. Weighed down by a nurses strike in California and Hawaii involving 31,000 workers, the sector lost 19,000 jobs last month, depriving the labor market of its most reliable ballast.\par \par \uc2 ''It's a pretty fragile labor market if we're depending on one industry to add all the jobs,'' said Kory Kantenga, head of economics for the Americas at \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The report is unlikely to shake the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's view ahead of its next meeting on March 17-18 that it can afford to take its time before cutting rates again. Yet it is certain to stoke divisions between officials there who appear highly concerned about the labor market's health and others who seem more focused on the risk posed by inflation, especially given the unrest in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 Brent crude, the international oil benchmark, settled on Friday at over $92 a barrel, up almost 30 percent over the past week. It was the biggest rise in the oil price since April 2020, during the coronavirus pandemic. The S&P 500 dropped 1.3 percent on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration blamed weak job numbers on severe winter storms and other factors and insisted that the president's economic agenda was working.\par \par \uc2 ''I think it's consistent with everything else we're seeing, which is the economy is really strong,'' \uc2 Kevin Hassett\uc2 , the director of the \uc2 White House National Economic Council\uc2 , said in an appearance on CNBC.\par \par \uc2 The February data did not show a more-than-typical weather effect.\par \par \uc2 Democrats pounced on the report, saying it was a sign that the economy could tip into a recession. Senator \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  of New York, called it a ''blaring alarm'' and said that ''the economy may go over the cliff.''\par \par \uc2 Economists cautioned not to read too much into one month's data and urged a wider view of the labor market that balanced the February job losses against the robust gains in January. Added together, the labor market gained 34,000 jobs in the first two months of the year.\par \par \uc2 ''You're seeing a labor market that's still kind of holding serve,'' said Stephen Juneau, an economist at \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''I think people got excited a little bit by January,'' he added, ''and this is just saying that, you know, the economy is maybe not turning all that quickly in either direction.''\par \par \uc2 Brandon Mabile, corporate business development director for Performance Contractors, a heavy industrial general contractor in Baton Rouge, La., said business slowed last year because of market conditions including tariff-driven price uncertainty. That depressed the company's need for more workers and led it to focus instead on keeping and training existing employees.\par \par \uc2 This year has been a different story. Projects that were postponed have started, and hiring has ballooned. In the past several months, Mr. Mabile has hired 2,000 construction workers, including welders, pipe fitters and carpenters, with most of that hiring coming in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 He estimated that he would hire up to 3,000 more employees this year, bringing his total work force to about 15,000.\par \par \uc2 ''We expect a continued increase in our hiring at least for the near future,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 There were other reassuring data points in the report on Friday. Wage growth remained solid, at 3.8 percent over the year, suggesting that hiring was not constrained solely by the supply of available workers even as the Trump administration's immigration policy has decreased the number of job seekers.\par \par \uc2 The share of people in their prime years who were working or looking for work fell slightly in February, to 83.9 percent, but was still hovering around post pandemic highs.\par \par \uc2 Although the unemployment rate rose, it did so marginally, implying that the economy does not need to add as many jobs to keep up with growth in the labor force. For some groups, including young people, the unemployment rate dropped.\par \par \uc2 Initial claims for unemployment insurance have also stayed low, indicating that employers overall are not laying off workers in large numbers despite some headline-grabbing jobs cuts at major companies.\par \par \uc2 Bob Funk Jr., the chief executive of Express Employment International, a staffing agency in Oklahoma City, said clients were ''feeling a little more assured about the economy'' and ''cautiously optimistic about where things are going.''\par \par \uc2 To the extent he has seen pullback in hiring, he said it had largely involved call centers, software companies and other employers that are more susceptible to artificial intelligence. He attributed any other reduction in hiring to a shortage in qualified workers and not to falling demand.\par \par \uc2 ''If they are slowing down their hiring, it's simply because they can't find the qualified talent,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Many analysts still expect the labor market to gain traction this year. Intense uncertainty around the Trump administration's trade policy and the war in Iran will probably ebb. Larger-than-normal tax refunds are likely to propel consumer spending, juicing the demand for labor at more companies.\par \par \uc2 ''It's a foggy road ahead -- where are we going?'' Mr. Juneau, the Bank of America economist, said. ''We think we're still going to see the labor market improve over the course of this year.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020260307em3700042\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex