\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Agency Launches Investigation Into Skiers' Guides\b0\par\par\uc2 By Christina Morales, Jill Cowan and Shawn Hubler \par \uc2 1,350 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 February 2026\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Officials in Nevada County, Calif., are investigating whether there could have been criminal negligence involved in the disaster that killed at least eight people.\par \par \uc2 As the authorities begin to piece together what led to the deaths of at least eight people in an avalanche in the snowy backcountry near Lake Tahoe this week, the risks taken by the skiers and their guides -- and who was responsible for fateful decisions -- are likely to be a key area of inquiry.\par \par \uc2 California's workplace safety agency, Cal-OSHA, said on Thursday that it had opened an investigation into Blackbird Mountain Guides, the company whose guides were leading the group.\par \par \uc2 The Nevada County Sheriff's Office said on Friday that it had also begun investigating the disaster to determine if there could have been criminal negligence involved.\par \par \uc2 ''It is a standard investigation,'' Ashley Cuadros, a spokeswoman for the sheriff's office, said in an email. ''It is too early to know if criminal charges will be applicable, as the investigation is preliminary and remains active and ongoing.''\par \par \uc2 The authorities have said that eight people died in the avalanche in the Sierra Nevada on Tuesday and that a ninth person is missing and presumed dead. Those nine included three experienced Blackbird guides, along with six of their clients, part of a group of mothers on the trip who knew one another and had skied regularly in the area.\par \par \uc2 Six other people in the party, including one other Blackbird employee, were rescued. The avalanche occurred near the end of the group's three-day trip to a backcountry lodge known as the Frog Lake huts, near Castle Peak.\par \par \uc2 The authorities have not yet been able to return to the area to recover the bodies of those who died. Nevada County officials announced on Friday that they were conducting aerial surveys to assess the avalanche site and determine if they might need to use explosives or other measures to prevent another avalanche before crews return. As of Friday afternoon, they had not yet sent search teams back in to find the bodies.\par \par \uc2 The authorities have released few details about what they believe occurred. However, forecasters and public safety officials had warned of a high risk of avalanches before the group began the trip, and additional warnings came during the trip itself.\par \par \uc2 Several experts and local officials cautioned that too little was yet known to assess whether Blackbird had been negligent.\par \par \uc2 In a statement on Thursday, Blackbird said that the guides on the trip were highly trained. ''It's too soon to draw conclusions, but investigations are underway,'' the company said.\par \par \uc2 The accident highlights the lack of regulation around backcountry skiing, whose participants are practicing a more dangerous form of the sport in remote areas, without the conventional avalanche control programs at resorts.\par \par \uc2 Beyond Cal-OSHA's regulations for workplace safety, oversight of the mountain and the outdoor excursions on it is limited, said Larry Heywood, a longtime snow and ski safety consultant who has lived and worked for decades in North Lake Tahoe. The mountain itself is mostly on federal land, he said, and the huts where the skiers stayed were on private property owned by a land trust.\par \par \uc2 He added that the \uc2 U.S. Forest Service\uc2  requires guides who operate on public land to obtain special use permits, and those typically require the operators to maintain safety plans and certify that their employees have appropriate qualifications and training. Skiers who venture into wilderness areas mostly do so at their own risk.\par \par \uc2 ''In winter, people go out in hazardous conditions all the time,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 The avalanche occurred along a Castle Peak path that is rated as complex under an Avalanche Terrain Exposure Scale, which takes into account various risk factors, according to information provided by the Truckee Donner Land Trust, the operator of the huts where the skiers stayed.\par \par \uc2 The trust's website also shows a separate, longer Johnson Canyon route that is rated as simple on the avalanche exposure scale, as well as a third path to the Donner Summit Rest Area that is rated as complex. The San Francisco Chronicle was first to report on the different routes, as well as the Nevada County investigation.\par \par \uc2 Experts cautioned that it would be premature to conclude why the group and the guides chose the route they took on Tuesday morning. There may have been mitigating factors that were considered or problems with other paths.\par \par \uc2 Bruce Tremper, a former director of the Utah Avalanche Center and the author of ''Staying Alive in Avalanche Terrain,'' said that there was always a risk of avalanches in the backcountry.\par \par \uc2 ''Even at low danger, you know you can find avalanches,'' Mr. Tremper said. He added that even with that inherent risk, backcountry skiing is always safest with a local, experienced mountain guide. Accidents do happen to guided groups, he said, ''but they're very, very rare.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Heywood said that backcountry guides typically require clients to sign liability waivers before their excursions begin, but the waivers are legally flimsy and often don't hold up in court.\par \par \uc2 He said it was clear that ''this is going to be a really big lawsuit.''\par \par \uc2 ''This was a guided group with a very experienced outfitter with very qualified guides,'' he added. ''This simply should not have happened if you're following the protocols of ski mountaineering. We don't know what led to this and what the decision-making process was yet. But something didn't go right.''\par \par \uc2 Jeffrey L. Kaloustian, a lawyer who handles ski-related personal injury cases in Grass Valley, Calif., said that liability waivers could not insulate the mountain guides from claims of reckless conduct or gross negligence, which does have a higher legal standard. What would constitute recklessness remains to be seen, he said, but ignoring or dismissing the seriousness of the avalanche hazard or weather forecasts could make a difference.\par \par \uc2 Avalanches happen with some regularity, he said, and they are one of the inherent risks of skiing, even in an organized ski area.\par \par \uc2 ''You hire a guide service, and you put the responsibility on the guide service of not just making sure that you are on the appropriate route to get from your starting point to your destination, but also to be assessing and mitigating risk along the way and making decisions,'' said Mr. Kaloustian, who also was a professional ski patroller at Palisades Tahoe and a former instructor, course director and mountain guide.\par \par \uc2 The circumstances around the avalanche are likely to revive a decades-long debate in California over how to regulate access to the outdoors.\par \par \uc2 While it might seem obvious to close off a whole mountain or national forest when there is extreme danger, it doesn't always work.\par \par \uc2 ''There's no gate big enough or net large enough,'' said Kurt Summers, who was a member of the Orange County Mountain Rescue Team for 15 years. He said that prevention through education was the best course of action. By the time rescuers can be called, he added, it is often too late.\par \par \uc2 David Reichel, executive director of the Sierra Avalanche Center, said that any proposal to limit public access to the backcountry when avalanche danger is high would face resistance. ''There is some base line level of risk all winter long, and access to the backcountry is pretty intrinsic to mountain communities, so I would imagine quite a bit of pushback against such ideas,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Hardy Bullock, a member of the Nevada County Board of Supervisors who represents the area where the avalanche took place, said on Wednesday that during the pandemic, thousands of additional visitors had descended on the Lake Tahoe area, including many who did not have much experience with extreme sports.\par \par \uc2 He said that backcountry travel is generally restricted only when there is extreme fire danger.\par \par \uc2 ''Anybody and their brother can go out there,'' he said. ''With that comes tremendous responsibility.''\par \par \uc2 Soumya Karlamangla and Cindy Hirschfeld contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Soumya Karlamangla and Cindy Hirschfeld contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020260221em2l0004k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
