\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 These Nurses Kept Picketing, Even as the Line Dwindled\b0\par\par\uc2 By Samantha Latson \par \uc2 920 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 February 2026\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 13\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Workers at four other New York hospitals voted to end their walkout while nurses at NewYork-Presbyterian/Columbia remained on the picket line.\par \par \uc2 The nurses, about 100 strong, stood outside the hospital bundled in red hats and scarves. ''She Works Hard for the Money'' blasted through three speakers posted on the street. The health care workers had been doing this for nearly 40 days at NewYork-Presbyterian/Columbia hospital. But something had changed.\par \par \uc2 As a strike by nurses at the hospital continued, the number on the picket line had dwindled by Wednesday and Thursday. The sidewalks outside the hospital were no longer crowded with a sea of hundreds and hundreds of nurses clad in red. And feelings of betrayal, frustration and restiveness were in the air.\par \par \uc2 The more than 4,000 nurses who work at NewYork-Presbyterian/Columbia, one of the city's largest hospitals, had remained on strike as they continued to fight to make sure they have job security and sufficient staffing. But they were alone.\par \par \uc2 Nurses at the two other health systems that had been affected by a strike that began Jan. 12 -- \uc2 Montefiore Medical Center\uc2  and three hospitals in the Mount Sinai system -- voted to end their strikes. But NewYork-Presbyterian nurses had remained off the job, with some feeling that hospital leaders had turned their backs on them. Others just wanted to return to their wards, longing to put an end to the conflict.\par \par \uc2 Early Friday morning, the nurses' union and hospital officials announced that they had reached a tentative deal to end the walkout. The health care workers are expected to vote in the coming days on the agreement. The union said that the deal would provide slightly stronger protections against layoffs and improved working conditions for nurses.\par \par \uc2 Cagatay Celik, a clinical nurse educator at NewYork-Presbyterian, said that while the nurses remain united, they were feeling the strain of the strike.\par \par \uc2 ''We are feeling a little bit of pressure,'' Mr. Celik said. ''This is now Week 6, and we are restless to get back to work -- back to our patients.''\par \par \uc2 A few blocks away, outside the children's hospital that is part of NewYork-Presbyterian/Columbia, the lyrics to Diana Ross's ''I'm Coming Out'' permeated the air as strikers stepped to the beat and waved their hands.\par \par \uc2 Keisha Bruington-Bragg, a nurse in the postpartum unit, danced on the picket line next to the D.J. She swayed her body to the rhythms to keep warm and to spark a chain reaction to lift her own spirits and those of her colleagues.\par \par \uc2 ''Being out here with my colleagues gives me energy and motivation to fight,'' Ms. Bruington-Bragg said. ''I feel dejected sometimes because it's sad we have to be out here.''\par \par \uc2 As the strike continued, many nurses were being stretched financially. Mr. Celik said that he started squirreling away money last summer, as the union made efforts to negotiate with the hospital.\par \par \uc2 He has a part-time job and took out a loan to provide a financial cushion during the strike. Mr. Celik said that he had encouraged his colleagues to follow suit or to find temporary work.\par \par \uc2 Kristina Merced, a nurse practitioner in the hospital's neonatal intensive care unit, said that going on strike without child care has been difficult for her family, which has had to draw down from savings.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Merced said that her husband, Joseph, who is self-employed, would normally watch their baby while she worked two or three days a week. Since the strike started, he has jumped in to take care of their 9-month-old son while Ms. Merced takes to the picket line every day.\par \par \uc2 Six weeks into the walkout, Ms. Merced said that she was in a groove and had sacrificed too much to turn back.\par \par \uc2 ''I never expected it to go this long, but at this point, I am extremely committed,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Sylvia Morales, who has been a neonatal nurse and instructor at NewYork-Presbyterian for more than 30 years, said that she had never imagined a walkout would stretch for weeks on end. Ms. Morales remembers when nurses staged a one-day strike in 1996.\par \par \uc2 She said that she feels betrayed and has found it hard to believe that nurses who provide specialized care to severely ill infants can be easily replaced with travel nurses.\par \par \uc2 ''I miss my work,'' she said. ''I want to get back there and take care of the babies.''\par \par \uc2 Nancy Hagans\uc2 , president of the \uc2 New York State Nurses Association\uc2 , had said that the nurses' union would not compromise on its demands for adequate staffing and job protections. Although the strike has lasted longer than anticipated, she said that the union had been equipped to fight until NewYork-Presbyterian ''does the right thing.''\par \par \uc2 When nurses at Montefiore and Mount Sinai staged a walkout in 2023 for three days, \uc2 Kenneth E. Raske\uc2 , president of the \uc2 Greater New York Hospital Association\uc2 , said that it caught everyone by surprise. The hospitals were determined to be prepared this time and made arrangements to avoid disruptions to patient care.\par \par \uc2 The nurses' union has said that its strike is motivated by concerns about the safety of nurses and their patients. Mr. Raske questioned that claim.\par \par \uc2 ''How could you say you're for patient safety and walk away from the job for six weeks?'' he said. ''I don't see how that adds up.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020260221em2l0004i\par }\page {\