\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Last Group of Striking N.Y.C. Nurses Reaches a Tentative Agreement\b0\par\par\uc2 By Joseph Goldstein \par \uc2 749 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 February 2026\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 13\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The agreement with NewYork-Presbyterian/Columbia Hospital must still be ratified by rank-and-file members.\par \par \uc2 After a strike that lasted nearly six weeks, the union representing nurses at NewYork-Presbyterian/Columbia Hospital said early Friday that it had reached a tentative agreement with the hospital, one of New York City's largest medical centers.\par \par \uc2 Should rank-and-file members approve the deal, one of the largest -- and longest -- nurses' strikes in recent decades would end. On Jan. 12, nearly 15,000 nurses at three of the city's major health systems walked out, citing grievances that included staffing levels and episodes of workplace violence.\par \par \uc2 More than 10,000 nurses at four hospitals -- \uc2 Montefiore Medical Center\uc2 , Mount Sinai Hospital and two other hospitals in the Mount Sinai system -- reached deals with their employers last week and have returned to work.\par \par \uc2 But more than 4,000 nurses who work at NewYork-Presbyterian/Columbia held out. They rejected a deal similar to what the Montefiore and Mount Sinai nurses had accepted, and they stayed on strike.\par \par \uc2 A NewYork-Presbyterian/Columbia spokeswoman, Angela Karafazli, confirmed on Friday that a tentative deal had been reached.\par \par \uc2 ''We are pleased to have reached a tentative settlement with \uc2 NYSNA\uc2 , through the mediator, that reflects our tremendous respect for our nurses,'' Ms. Karafazli said in a statement early Friday, referring to the nurses' union, the \uc2 New York State Nurses Association\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 By holding out for an extra week, the NewYork-Presbyterian nurses appear to have won slightly better terms. According to the union, the tentative deal would allow the union to put its staffing complaints in front of an arbitrator more quickly. That could mean better working conditions and staffing levels, according to the nurses.\par \par \uc2 The union also said the tentative deal gives it more control over where in the hospital newly hired nurses are assigned. And the agreement contains slightly stronger layoff protections, according to the union.\par \par \uc2 ''This has been a long, hard fight, but we are proud of what we achieved,'' said Beth Loudin, a nurse who led the union bargaining committee at \uc2 NewYork-Presbyterian Hospital\uc2 . ''With the strength of our nurses and the support of our community and allies, we showed the hospital that nurses will not compromise on our patients' care.''\par \par \uc2 The nurses at the hospital are expected to vote on whether to ratify the deal over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 Nurses who work in the \uc2 Mount Sinai Health System\uc2  and at Montefiore had voted overwhelmingly last week in favor of a deal that had been put forward by a mediator, according to the union.\par \par \uc2 The length of the strike came as a surprise to many nurses on the picket line. When they last walked off the job, in early 2023, hospitals quickly agreed to their demands, which focused on staffing levels. That strike lasted three days. But the nurses had more leverage then, and the hospitals were caught unprepared, with few executives suspecting a walkout.\par \par \uc2 The latest strike also revealed divisions within the union. Last week, Ms. Loudin and other nurses sought disciplinary action against union leaders, claiming that they had tried to force an earlier deal on members.\par \par \uc2 ''For a month and a half, through some of the harshest weather this city has seen in years, nurses at NYP showed this city that they won't make any compromises to patient care,'' \uc2 Nancy Hagans\uc2 , the union's president, said in a statement on Friday, referring to NewYork-Presbyterian nurses.\par \par \uc2 ''They stood in the cold, snow, ice and wind, along with their union siblings, fighting back management's attempts to cut corners on care and secured contracts that improve enforceable safe staffing ratios, improve protections from workplace violence and maintain health benefits with no additional out-of-pocket costs for frontline nurses,'' Ms Hagans said.\par \par \uc2 Nurses have long said that some hospital units in the city are chronically understaffed, leading to inferior care. The 2023 strike resulted in improved nurse-to-patient staffing ratios.\par \par \uc2 Hospitals agreed three years ago to pay extra wages to nurses if hospitals left units insufficiently staffed. That has resulted in significant payouts. Arbitrators have awarded nurses at three hospitals in the Mount Sinai system more than $6 million since the start of 2024 based on evidence that units at the hospitals did not have enough staff, according to the union.\par \par \uc2 Hospitals have chafed at the awards, creating a sticking point in negotiations.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020260221em2l0004h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
