\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Apple\uc2  Is Sued Over Allegations Of Sharing Child Abuse Images\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kalley Huang \par \uc2 735 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 February 2026\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 5\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The state's attorney general said in a lawsuit filed on Thursday that the company declined to use tools that recognize the material stored on iCloud.\par \par \uc2 The West Virginia attorney general sued \uc2 Apple\uc2  on Thursday over allegations the company knowingly allowed its iCloud service to be used to store and share child sexual abuse material. It is believed to be the first lawsuit filed by a state or federal agency against \uc2 Apple\uc2  over the issue.\par \par \uc2 In a 52-page complaint, the state said that by declining to use certain tools that recognize photos and videos containing child sexual abuse material, \uc2 Apple\uc2  facilitated the spread of the material and violated local consumer protection law.\par \par \uc2 ''\uc2 Apple\uc2  is using the cloak of privacy in order to mitigate its duties to children and families in this country,'' John B. McCuskey, the state's attorney general, said in an interview. The images need to be removed, he added, and ''\uc2 Apple\uc2  needs to be working with law enforcement to make sure that everyone that owns them is prosecuted to the fullest extent of the law.''\par \par \uc2 The West Virginia lawsuit spotlights concerns that the privacy of \uc2 Apple\uc2 's iCloud allows illegal material to be stored and shared more easily than on social media apps like \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and \uc2 Instagram\uc2 . The suit seeks monetary damages and changes to \uc2 Apple\uc2 's practices for detecting child sexual abuse material and designing safe products.\par \par \uc2 ''At \uc2 Apple\uc2 , protecting the safety and privacy of our users, especially children, is central to what we do,'' \uc2 Apple\uc2  said in a statement. ''We are innovating every day to combat ever-evolving threats and maintain the safest, most trusted platform for kids.'' The company added that it offered parental controls, including a feature that warns children if they receive or try to send content with nudity.\par \par \uc2 Apple\uc2  has been sued before by victims for failing to limit child sexual abuse material on iCloud. In December 2024, a group of victims sought more than $1.2 billion in damages from the company, which filed a dismissal motion that is still pending.\par \par \uc2 In August 2024, a 9-year-old girl in North Carolina sued \uc2 Apple\uc2  after strangers sent her child sexual abuse videos through iCloud and encouraged her to film and upload her own nude videos. That case was largely dismissed last year.\par \par \uc2 ''We need somebody to hold them accountable and make them answer the question of whether or not they believe this belongs on this platform,'' said Sarah Gardner, the founder of Heat Initiative, a child safety group that helped bring the lawsuit in December 2024.\par \par \uc2 For years, \uc2 Apple\uc2 's reports of child sexual abuse material have been a tiny fraction of the totals reported by its peers. In 2023, for example, \uc2 Meta\uc2  made more than 30 million reports of suspected sexual abuse material to the \uc2 National Center for Missing & Exploited Children\uc2 , a federal clearinghouse. \uc2 Google\uc2  made more than one million reports. \uc2 Apple\uc2  made 267.\par \par \uc2 West Virginia's lawsuit is the latest example of a novel legal strategy against big technology companies. Section 230 of the Communications Decency Act of 1996 shields companies from legal liability for what their users post. But recent lawsuits have argued that technology companies are subject to personal injury liability for creating defective products that harm users. \uc2 Meta\uc2  and \uc2 YouTube\uc2  are on trial in California state court, accused of encouraging social media addiction.\par \par \uc2 In 2009, \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  helped create a tool for recognizing photos and videos containing child sexual abuse material, called PhotoDNA, which companies including \uc2 Google\uc2  and \uc2 Dropbox\uc2  then adopted. \uc2 Apple\uc2  declined to use PhotoDNA.\par \par \uc2 In 2019, an investigation by \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  found that technology companies failed to police abusive material on their platforms. Reporting by The Times led \uc2 Eric Friedman\uc2 , an executive responsible for fraud protection at \uc2 Apple\uc2 , to tell a colleague that he thought the company was underreporting child sexual abuse material.\par \par \uc2 ''We are the greatest platform for distributing child porn,'' Mr. Friedman said in a text message in 2020. He said that was because the company had put a priority on privacy over safety.\par \par \uc2 In 2021, \uc2 Apple\uc2  introduced its own system for detecting child sexual abuse material, using a technique called NeuralHash. But cybersecurity experts warned that the system could endanger user privacy and give governments access to iPhones. Shortly after, \uc2 Apple\uc2  dropped its plans for the system.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020260220em2k0003z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
