\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Musk Proposes New Location Of A.I. Factory: The Moon\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kate Conger and Ryan Mac \par \uc2 740 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 February 2026\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 5\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 In a meeting with employees at his company \uc2 xAI\uc2 , Mr. Musk revealed a vision for a facility that includes a giant catapult to launch his satellites into space.\par \par \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  told employees at \uc2 xAI\uc2 , his artificial intelligence company, on Tuesday evening that the company needed a factory on the moon to build A.I. satellites and a massive catapult to launch them into space.\par \par \uc2 Inspired by the billionaire's love of science fiction, the space catapult would be called a mass driver, and would be part of an imagined lunar facility that manufactured satellites to provide the computing power for the company's A.I.\par \par \uc2 ''You have to go to the moon,'' Mr. Musk said during an all-hands meeting, which was heard by \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 . The move would help \uc2 xAI\uc2  harness more power than other companies to build its A.I., he said.\par \par \uc2 ''It's difficult to imagine what an intelligence of that scale would think about, but it's going to be incredibly exciting to see it happen,'' he added.\par \par \uc2 Last week, Mr. Musk said he was merging \uc2 xAI\uc2  with his rocket business, \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 , to facilitate his plans to create A.I. data centers in outer space. Now that vision has expanded to include the lunar facility, though he did not say in his hourlong talk, which also featured remarks from other executives, how it could be built.\par \par \uc2 Those two arms of Mr. Musk's business empire are merging as \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  prepares an initial public offering, which could come as early as June. A representative for \uc2 xAI\uc2  did not respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Musk's fixation with the moon is a recent one. Since founding \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  in 2002, he has said making humanity multiplanetary, first by establishing a colony on Mars, was the company's raison d'\u234\'00\'EAtre. But in recent months, he has posted frequently on X, his social media platform, about the company's new focus: the moon.\par \par \uc2 Two former \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  executives told The Times, on the condition of anonymity because they were not authorized to speak publicly about corporate plans, that the moon had never been a main focus of the company.\par \par \uc2 In his remarks on Tuesday, Mr. Musk described the moon as a steppingstone to Mars. First, he said, the company would build ''a self-sustaining city on the moon,'' then travel to Mars and finally explore star systems in search of aliens.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Musk also talked about more earthly concerns with workers. Alongside executives from X, which he merged with \uc2 xAI\uc2  last year, Mr. Musk described the social network's progress and where he hopes it will go.\par \par \uc2 X has about 600 million monthly active users, Mr. Musk said, a metric that The Times was not able to verify. When he acquired the company, then known as \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , in 2022, it said it had 237.8 million daily active users who were able to view ads.\par \par \uc2 ''Most people only occasionally come to the X app when there's some major world event,'' Mr. Musk said. But he said the addition of more services in the coming months -- such as a banking feature called X Money and a stand-alone chat app -- would make X more appealing.\par \par \uc2 ''We'll obviously give people reasons, compelling reasons, to use the app every day and have, my expectation is, well over a billion daily active users,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Musk has made bold and sometimes inaccurate predictions about when he will be able to introduce new technologies. In 2016, for instance, he said \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  would send its first cargo to Mars by 2018, a mission that has yet to materialize.\par \par \uc2 But he told employees that he expected \uc2 xAI\uc2  to continue to grow quickly, even as he hinted at the loss of early-stage employees and a possible reorganization of the company.\par \par \uc2 ''If you're moving faster than anyone else in any given technology arena, you will be the leader, and \uc2 xAI\uc2  is moving faster than any other company -- no one's even close,'' Mr. Musk said. ''Because we've reached a certain scale, we're organizing the company to be more effective at this scale. And actually, when this happens, there's some people who are better suited for the early stages of a company and less suited for the later stages.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020260213em2d00030\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
