\par\par \uc2 STOCKS &AMP; BONDS\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Wall Street Wobbles in the Wake of a Surprising Jobs Report\b0\par\par\uc2 By The Associated Press \par \uc2 694 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 February 2026\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks felt both the upside and downside Wednesday of a surprisingly strong report that said the nation's unemployment rate improved last month.\par \par \uc2 After initially rising toward a high, the S&P 500 flipped between gains and losses before finishing with a minuscule dip of less than 0.1 percent. The Dow Jones industrial average dropped 66 points, or 0.1 percent, and the Nasdaq composite fell 0.2 percent. Both also erased early gains.\par \par \uc2 Treasury yields, meanwhile, remained higher in the bond market after the Labor Department said U.S. employers added 130,000 jobs to their payrolls last month, more than economists expected. That helped calm worries from a day earlier, when a discouraging report suggested spending by U.S. households, the main engine of the economy, may be stalling.\par \par \uc2 On one hand, the strong data on jobs raises hopes that the U.S. economy can remain solid and keep driving big profits for companies. Stocks in the energy and raw-material industries jumped to some of the bigger gains in the S&P 500, for example, and their profits tend to be closely tied to the health of the economy.\par \par \uc2 Exxon Mobil\uc2  climbed 2.6 percent. \uc2 Smurfit Westrock\uc2  jumped 9.9 percent even though the packaging company reported a weaker profit for the latest quarter than analysts expected. It gave financial targets for the next five years that some analysts found encouraging.\par \par \uc2 But on the other hand for the broad stock market, the stronger-than-expected jobs data could also keep the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  on hold when it comes to cuts to interest rates. And higher rates can drag on prices for stocks and all kinds of other investments.\par \par \uc2 After Wednesday's report showed the tick down for the U.S. unemployment rate, traders pushed back their bets for when the Fed could begin cutting interest rates again, according to data from \uc2 CME Group\uc2 . The bets slid further into the summer, after a new Fed chair is set to take the helm.\par \par \uc2 If the jobs report had shown a rise in the unemployment rate or a worsening job market, that could have pushed the Fed to resume its cuts more quickly.\par \par \uc2 Lower rates would stimulate the economy and financial markets, though at the cost of potentially worsening inflation. The next monthly update on inflation at the consumer level arrives on Friday, and it will likely be another big influence on the Fed's plans.\par \par \uc2 After the jobs report, the yield on the 10-year Treasury edged up to 4.17 percent from 4.16 percent late Tuesday. The two-year Treasury yield, which moves more closely with expectations for Fed moves, climbed more. It rose to 3.51 percent from 3.45 percent.\par \par \uc2 To be sure, all is still not perfectly clear for the U.S. economy. Wednesday's report included major revisions, which said employers added just 181,000 jobs for all of last year. That's less than a third of the previously reported 584,000, and it's the weakest showing for a year since 2020, when Covid shut the economy.\par \par \uc2 The overall jobs report nevertheless looked to be an encouraging signal for the economy.\par \par \uc2 ''We all knew there would be downward revisions, but these were better than expected,'' \uc2 Brian Jacobsen\uc2 , chief economic strategist at Annex Wealth Management, said of the markdowns for 2025.\par \par \uc2 On Wall Street, \uc2 Robinhood Markets\uc2  fell 8.8 percent even though the trading and investment app reported a stronger profit for the latest quarter than analysts expected. Its revenue fell short of forecasts, and analysts highlighted \uc2 Robinhood\uc2 's forecast for expenses in 2026, along with concerns about how long a slowdown in crypto trading will last.\par \par \uc2 Crypto prices have plunged recently, and Bitcoin's price fell toward $67,000 Wednesday. It's lost close to half its value since setting a record in October.\par \par \uc2 Moderna\uc2  dropped 3.5 percent after saying the F.D.A. is refusing to consider its application for a new flu vaccine made with Nobel Prize-winning mRNA technology.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020260212em2c0004w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
