\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Restoration Of Funding For Gateway Is Delayed\b0\par\par\uc2 By Patrick McGeehan \par \uc2 681 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 February 2026\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A federal judge had ordered the funding for a $16 billion tunnel project restored last week, but on Monday granted a temporary stay after the federal government appealed her decision.\par \par \uc2 As dozens of newly laid-off union members exhorted President Trump to restore funding to the $16 billion Gateway rail tunnel project on Monday, a federal judge ruled that the flow of money would not have to be restarted before Thursday evening.\par \par \uc2 Judge Jeannette Vargas of Federal District Court in Manhattan had ordered the federal Department of Transportation on Friday night to end a four-month suspension of funding for the tunnel, which would connect New York City and New Jersey at the middle of the busy Northeast Corridor rail route.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, Judge Vargas denied the federal government's request for a stay of that order, but issued a temporary stay until 5 p.m. Thursday to give the government time to file an appeal in the U.S. Court of Appeals for the Second Circuit.\par \par \uc2 That decision could leave most of the work on Gateway, which Democratic officials say is the most important infrastructure project in the nation, at a standstill for at least three more days. The \uc2 Gateway Development Commission\uc2 , which manages the tunnel project, laid off about 1,000 workers on Friday after it exhausted a bank line of credit it had used to cover expenses, said Thomas Prendergast, the commission's chief executive.\par \par \uc2 The Laborers' Union, which represents many of those workers, bused dozens of them to an idle work site in North Bergen, N.J., on Monday morning. There, a massive tunnel-boring machine lay in pieces near the rock wall that would serve as the western mouth of the tunnel.\par \par \uc2 ''Release the funds now!'' the workers chanted as union leaders and two Democratic elected officials -- Senator \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  of New York and Gov. \uc2 Mikie Sherrill\uc2  of New Jersey -- stood on a makeshift stage and blamed Mr. Trump for the layoffs.\par \par \uc2 Manuel Amador Jr., business manager for Local 472 of the Laborers' Union, called the matter a ''vendetta'' and said that ''working people should never be pawns'' in a political chess match.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Sherrill, who called herself a ''tunnel-obsessed'' governor, said, ''All of you know that this is a special job; this is a generational job.''\par \par \uc2 She added, ''I will not rest until you guys are back at work.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Schumer, the Senate minority leader and a longtime champion of the Gateway project, said the chants ''came from the right people'' because many of the construction workers probably had supported Mr. Trump. He said he believed the workers' anger would have ''more effect against Trump than just about anything else.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Schumer declined to discuss details of his conversations with Mr. Trump, which reportedly involved demands that Mr. Schumer support the renaming of Washington Dulles International Airport and Pennsylvania Station in Manhattan after the president in exchange for restoration of Gateway's funding.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump told reporters aboard Air Force One on Friday that Mr. Schumer had been the one who suggested renaming Penn Station after the president. On social media, Mr. Schumer called that claim an ''absolute lie.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Schumer has repeatedly said that Mr. Trump alone has the power to restore the funding to Gateway and end the costly pause in construction.\par \par \uc2 Last week, Mr. Prendergast said that the longer the work was delayed, the more it would cost to restart it. He said more than $1 billion had already been spent on the project, including on the fabrication of two boring machines in Germany.\par \par \uc2 He declined on Monday to discuss the effects of the shutdown or when it might end. Molly Beckhardt, a spokeswoman for the development commission, said that ''construction will remain suspended until federal funding disbursements resume.'' She added that the commission was continuing ''to pursue all avenues to regain access to this urgent project.''\par \par \uc2 The development commission also sued the federal government over the funding suspension in a federal claims court in Washington last week.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020260211em2b0003i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
