\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 New Hollywood Labor Talks, With Strikes a Painful Memory\b0\par\par\uc2 By John Koblin \par \uc2 731 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 February 2026\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The last time writers and actors negotiated contracts, in 2023, dual strikes froze the industry.\par \par \uc2 It has been just 26 months since some of the most grueling strikes in Hollywood history came to an end.\par \par \uc2 Now comes the possibility that it will happen all over again.\par \par \uc2 The contracts that ended the strikes in 2023 are nearing their expiration dates -- May 1 for writers and June 30 for actors. On Monday, negotiations will begin with the studios for new deals.\par \par \uc2 The sides will be negotiating in a Hollywood far different from 2023. Production has slowed significantly industrywide, as many entertainment companies struggle to adjust to the streaming world. Work has dried up for many actors, writers and directors. At the same time, the rise of generative artificial intelligence has become more central.\par \par \uc2 The negotiations on Monday will be between \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2 , the union that represents thousands of actors; and the leaders of the \uc2 Alliance of Motion Picture and Television Producers\uc2 , the studios' bargaining organization. Numerous bargaining sessions are expected in the coming weeks.\par \par \uc2 ''We're doing everything under our power to keep our members working,'' Duncan Crabtree-Ireland, the chief negotiator of the actors' union, said in an interview. ''That's what they want -- that's what the whole industry wants. Having said that, we are not going to accept a deal that's unfair to our membership.''\par \par \uc2 In a statement, the studio alliance said it was ''optimistic that, together, we can reach a fair deal that reflects our shared commitment to supporting our industry's talented performers and promoting long-term stability.''\par \par \uc2 In March, the Writers Guild of America will begin its discussions with the studios, and the directors' union -- which did not strike last time -- will get in the bargaining room later in the spring.\par \par \uc2 The dual strikes by the writers' and actors' unions in 2023 were a first in Hollywood since the early 1960s. The industry's production lines ground to a halt for many months, and the economic fallout was significant.\par \par \uc2 Compensation and general working conditions were among the major issues last time. Artificial intelligence was also an issue, and it will come up again this time: Mr. Crabtree-Ireland said the union would push for additional guardrails around its use.\par \par \uc2 Sean Astin\uc2 , the ''Rudy'' and ''Lord of the Rings'' actor, who replaced Fran Drescher as the president of \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2  last year, said streaming residuals, a type of royalty, would be an additional matter on the bargaining table, as would other ''meat and potatoes'' issues.\par \par \uc2 ''Our members' lived experience is no longer properly reflected in the contract, and so those things have to be brought into alignment,'' Mr. Astin said.\par \par \uc2 The writers' union has not yet detailed its top priorities, but replenishing the writers' health care reserves is expected to be one of them. Marguerite MacIntyre, a board member of the Writers Guild of America, said last year that ''the question of how to continue to fund our health care'' would be a major element of the labor talks. Michele Mulroney, the president of the Writers Guild's western branch, said last year that health care would be ''a headline issue.''\par \par \uc2 The studios are expected to have an ask of their own: five-year contracts, two more years than has been typical. The studios are expected to point to the stability of other industries with longer labor contracts, including the \uc2 National Football League\uc2  (10-year deals) and the longshoremen's union and their employers (six years), according to two people with knowledge of the plans.\par \par \uc2 Union leaders have said in the past that three-year contracts provide vital flexibility to respond to technological changes. Christopher Nolan, the heavyweight Hollywood director and new president of the \uc2 Directors Guild of America\uc2 , told the trade publication Deadline last week, ''I don't think a five-year extension is in any way a realistic proposal.''\par \par \uc2 The studio alliance will also be led by a new head negotiator, Greg Hessinger. Mr. Crabtree-Ireland, the \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2  negotiator, said that he knew Mr. Hessinger when he worked with the actors' union decades ago, and that he would bring ''a likely vibe shift.''\par \par \uc2 ''I'm cautiously optimistic that this is a positive step in the relationship between the companies and the unions in the entertainment industry,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020260210em2a0003w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
