\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 2 Unions Scold Airline Leaders Over Operation Of the Company\b0\par\par\uc2 By Niraj Chokshi \par \uc2 708 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 February 2026\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The unions representing pilots and flight attendants have in recent days publicly criticized American for falling behind Delta Air Lines and United Airlines.\par \par \uc2 Pilots and flight attendants at \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  have become so fed up with the company's leadership that their frustrations have spilled into public view in recent days.\par \par \uc2 The union that represents the airline's 28,000 flight attendants said on Monday that its board had issued a unanimous vote of no confidence in the company's chief executive, \uc2 Robert Isom\uc2 . And on Friday, the union that represents 16,000 pilots asked to meet with the board of directors, saying American was ''on an underperforming path and has failed to define an identity or a strategy to correct course.''\par \par \uc2 The rebukes are notable because neither union is in the middle of negotiating a new contract with the airline, when workers and managers are often at loggerheads about many issues.\par \par \uc2 American has lost substantial ground to \uc2 Delta Air Lines\uc2  and \uc2 United Airlines\uc2  in recent years. In 2024 and 2025, Delta earned about 56 percent of the U.S. airline industry's total profits, according to data from \uc2 S&P Capital IQ\uc2 . United earned about 43 percent, while American came in third at 6 percent despite being the largest carrier based on the number of flights and passengers. Pilots and flight attendants at all three airlines earn a share of company profits.\par \par \uc2 Aviation experts say Delta and United are doing better partly because they operate at airports and on routes that are more lucrative. But analysts also said American had not invested as much as the other two airlines in premium seats, which have become much more popular in recent years and are a major source of profits.\par \par \uc2 ''From abysmal profits earned to operational failures that have frontline workers sleeping on floors, this airline must course-correct before it falls even further behind,'' \uc2 Julie Hedrick\uc2 , the president of the flight attendants' union, the \uc2 Association of Professional Flight Attendants\uc2 , said in the statement. The union said it was the first time it had formally expressed a lack of confidence in the airline's chief executive.\par \par \uc2 In its letter, the pilots' union, the \uc2 Allied Pilots Association\uc2 , noted the company's lagging performance and said it had raised concerns about operational problems with management over the past year. American ''continues to struggle under predictable stressors, exposing systemic weaknesses in preparation, execution and decision making,'' the union said.\par \par \uc2 American, which is based in Fort Worth, was particularly hard hit by the winter storm that wreaked havoc across the eastern United States in January. The company canceled more than 9,000 flights, making it the airline's worst-ever weather-related disruption. American said it had suffered much more than other airlines because the weather had affected five of its nine airport hubs.\par \par \uc2 In a response to the pilots' union, Mr. Isom said on Saturday that he and the rest of the airline's board had discussed the group's message ''at length'' and agreed that he should meet with the union's leadership.\par \par \uc2 ''I appreciate the opportunity to address the items outlined in your letter and the specifics you relayed to me personally regarding our handling of winter storms Fern and Gianna; American's focus on pilot attendance policies; and our business plan to return American to its rightful place atop the industry,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 In response to a request for comment, American referred to public comments that Mr. Isom had made in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 On a call last month to discuss the airline's quarterly performance, he said American was working to provide consistent, good customer service; make the best use of its hub airports and planes; improve its loyalty program; and increase revenue.\par \par \uc2 ''While this has been a multiyear effort, 2026 will be the year these efforts start to bear fruit,'' Mr. Isom said. ''We're off to a fast start based on the booking trends we've observed in January, all-time records for the first three weeks of the year,'' he added.\par \par \uc2 American is also investing heavily in lounges, premium seats and other initiatives to attract and retain wealthier customers.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020260210em2a0003u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
