\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Nike\uc2  and Trump's Supporters Have Been Heading Toward a Clash\b0\par\par\uc2 By Rebecca Davis O'Brien and Kim Bhasin \par \uc2 1,303 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 February 2026\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The chair of the \uc2 Equal Employment Opportunity Commission\uc2  has long opposed diversity initiatives like those that \uc2 Nike\uc2  embraced.\par \par \uc2 Before Andrea Lucas joined the \uc2 Equal Employment Opportunity Commission\uc2  in 2020, she worked at a white-shoe law firm where she advised companies on the requirements of federal labor law, including in the growing area of diversity, equity and inclusion initiatives.\par \par \uc2 At the time, companies were eager to adopt such practices, some of which she considered ''extremely legally risky,'' Ms. Lucas, now the chair of the commission, said in a recent interview.\par \par \uc2 The legal landscape has changed. In the second Trump administration, with Ms. Lucas at the helm of the agency charged with enforcing federal labor laws, D.E.I. has become not a goal but a target.\par \par \uc2 ''That was a very bad gamble that many companies took,'' Ms. Lucas said.\par \par \uc2 Nike\uc2 , the world's largest sportswear company, has learned this the hard way. On Wednesday, the commission revealed in a statement and in filings in federal court that it had been investigating \uc2 Nike\uc2 's efforts to increase racial diversity in its employee and leadership ranks. Ms. Lucas has said \uc2 Nike\uc2  may have violated civil rights laws by discriminating against white people.\par \par \uc2 The investigation has set up a showdown between the Trump administration and a prominent company over divergent interpretations of civil rights laws and, more fundamentally, over questions of race and fairness in the workplace.\par \par \uc2 Nike\uc2  said it had responded to the commission's subpoena and requests for information and was surprised by this week's public escalation. ''We will continue our attempt to cooperate with the E.E.O.C.,'' \uc2 Nike\uc2  said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 A spokesman for the E.E.O.C. declined to comment on the investigation, beyond the papers filed in federal court.\par \par \uc2 The investigation, made public through a filing in federal court, was opened by Ms. Lucas herself in May 2024, when she was the lone Republican on the commission. It is unusual for the commission to investigate a company proactively rather than in response to a complaint from an employee. The move puts Ms. Lucas and the E.E.O.C. at the vanguard of the Trump administration's efforts to quash D.E.I. initiatives and punish organizations that champion them.\par \par \uc2 In \uc2 Nike\uc2 , Ms. Lucas has singled out a globally recognized brand that has for decades been connected with issues of discrimination and social justice. \uc2 Nike\uc2 , named after the Greek goddess of victory, has nearly $50 billion in revenue and is a cultural force that courses through sports and style, fusing fashion and streetwear across pop culture.\par \par \uc2 Nike\uc2  has a close relationship with the Black community that has, in many ways, driven its brand over the years. Michael Jordan's Air Jordan shoes have been top sellers for 40 years. Mr. Jordan's triumphs on the basketball court and his international stardom redefined \uc2 Nike\uc2  sneakers as an emblem of aspiration and an American cultural export.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Jordan has rarely taken public stances on political issues. ''Republicans buy sneakers, too,'' he once said.\par \par \uc2 Nike\uc2 's younger promoters have been less reticent. The tennis superstar Serena Williams fights for gender pay equity and maternal health matters, and the basketball player Maya Moore leads a justice reform campaign. \uc2 Nike\uc2 's biggest active star, LeBron James, has spoken out against voter suppression and racial inequality.\par \par \uc2 Nike\uc2  has leaned into activism over the past decade as well. \uc2 Mark Parker\uc2 , who spent 14 years as the company's chief executive before becoming executive chairman in 2020, addressed employees during times of national distress, such as after the deadly white nationalist rally in Charlottesville, Va., in 2017.\par \par \uc2 In 2016, he sent an impassioned letter to employees condemning discrimination and pledging that \uc2 Nike\uc2  would become more diverse and inclusive. ''I'm proud that \uc2 Nike\uc2  takes a stand on issues that impact all of us, our athletes and society as a whole,'' Mr. Parker wrote. ''We stand against bigotry. We stand for racial justice.''\par \par \uc2 Nike\uc2  made its loudest statement two years later when it made Colin Kaepernick, the football star who knelt during the national anthem to protest police brutality, the face of an advertising campaign.\par \par \uc2 ''Believe in something,'' read the ad, ''even if it means sacrificing everything.''\par \par \uc2 President Trump called it ''a terrible message.''\par \par \uc2 Since Mr. Parker stepped aside, \uc2 Nike\uc2  has teamed up with civil rights organizations such as the \uc2 NAACP Legal Defense and Educational Fund Inc.\uc2  and the \uc2 National Urban League\uc2 . When George Floyd was murdered by a police officer in Minneapolis in 2020 and protests spread across the country, \uc2 Nike\uc2  spoke out.\par \par \uc2 ''We know Black lives matter,'' Mr. Parker's successor, \uc2 John Donahoe\uc2 , said while committing $40 million to support the Black community. ''The \uc2 Nike\uc2  family can always do more but will never stop striving to role model how a diverse company acts.''\par \par \uc2 Under Mr. Donahoe, \uc2 Nike\uc2  embarked on a five-year plan to sculpt a more diverse work force. The company tied some executive compensation to diversity goals and set representation targets in the United States for women and racial and ethnic minorities.\par \par \uc2 But last year, after Mr. Trump returned to office, Nike decided not to release an annual corporate sustainability report that was expected to provide updates on those diversity initiatives. The company said at the time that it was still committed to those objectives.\par \par \uc2 The commission's investigation into Nike's diversity initiatives is an early test for Elliott Hill, the company's current chief executive, who came out of retirement to return to Nike's headquarters in Beaverton, Ore., 15 months ago. He is trying to pull the business out of a sales slump. Mr. Hill has worked to refocus Nike on sports, speed up product development and get rid of old inventory to make way for new designs.\par \par \uc2 According to court filings, the commission sent requests to Nike starting in December 2024 seeking information about any race-based ''quotas'' used to increase hiring diversity and details about the targets of recent layoffs, among other things.\par \par \uc2 Nike responded with objections and some information, but not all of it, the commission said in a court filing Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 On Sept. 30, the director of the commission's St. Louis field office issued a subpoena seeking more detailed information. A week later, Nike filed a petition to change or revoke it. In early January, the commission gave Nike a Jan. 26 deadline.\par \par \uc2 Nike's response, according to the commission, did not include full information for some of the requests, including numbers about racial representation or details about how executive compensation was set and vacancies filled.\par \par \uc2 One practice the commission asked about was a so-called diverse slates hiring protocol that, at Nike, created candidate pools including at least two women and one racial or ethnic minority.\par \par \uc2 In its January response to the subpoena, Nike said the protocol was ''designed to broaden the funnel of qualified candidates'' applying to jobs, which the company said was in line with federal guidance ''to address substantial disparity'' in minority groups or among genders. The company also said no diverse slates practices were currently in effect.\par \par \uc2 Nike said it planned to send additional responses by the week of Feb. 9.\par \par \uc2 Jenny R. Yang, a Democrat and former chair of the E.E.O.C., said the subpoena was broad and possibly exceeded the commission's authority.\par \par \uc2 ''Simply targeting practices that are designed to promote equal employment opportunity, like aspirational goals, raises concerns,'' said Ms. Yang, who was on the commission from 2013 to 2018. She added that the investigation appeared to be an attempt to intimidate companies.\par \par \uc2 ''Instead of helping employers comply'' with federal civil rights laws, she said, ''they just want to investigate them.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020260207em270003u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
