\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Google\uc2  Workers Protest Its Ties To ICE Drive On Immigrants\b0\par\par\uc2 By Tripp Mickle \par \uc2 678 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 February 2026\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 More than 800 employees delivered a petition to management, condemning the Trump administration's use of \uc2 Google\uc2  technology in immigration enforcement.\par \par \uc2 After federal immigration agents killed Alex Pretti in Minneapolis, \uc2 Google\uc2  employees lit up the company's internal message boards with calls for the company to respond.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, more than 800 employees called on management in a petition to be transparent about how \uc2 Google\uc2 's technology supports federal immigration agencies and urged the company to stop doing business with those organizations. The petition said they were ''appalled by the violence'' and ''paramilitary-style raids'' by immigration agents, which they accused \uc2 Google\uc2  of aiding.\par \par \uc2 They also asked the company to take safety measures to protect employees after a reported attempt by \uc2 Immigration and Customs Enforcement\uc2  agents to enter the company's campus in Cambridge, Mass.\par \par \uc2 The petition signaled a revival of employee activism at \uc2 Google\uc2  and across Silicon Valley after years of relative calm. Tech workers, who have largely stayed quiet as executives cozied up to the Trump administration, are beginning to push some of the world's biggest companies to pressure the White House to change its policies.\par \par \uc2 While signed by a small fraction of the company's roughly 190,000 workers, the new petition echoes turmoil at \uc2 Google\uc2  in 2018, after workers walked out over the company's handling of sexual harassment and then protested its involvement in a Pentagon program that used artificial intelligence to improve drone strikes.\par \par \uc2 In the years since, \uc2 Google\uc2 's management limited employees' access to internal documents, scaled back all-hands meetings and cracked down on dissent. It fired 28 workers two years ago for protesting its cloud computing contract with the Israeli government.\par \par \uc2 As a result, some employees have become more hesitant to challenge management, said Matthew Tschiegg, an engineer on the cloud computing team who signed the petition. But many employees still believe in the company's informal corporate motto: ''Don't be evil.''\par \par \uc2 ''They've scratched or scrubbed that motto, but the ethos lives deep,'' said Mr. Tschiegg, who has worked at \uc2 Google\uc2  for more than a decade.\par \par \uc2 Since President Trump returned to the White House, Silicon Valley executives and investors -- including \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , who leads \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  and \uc2 Tesla\uc2 ; \uc2 Tim Cook\uc2 , chief executive of \uc2 Apple\uc2 ; and \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2 , chief executive of \uc2 Meta\uc2  -- have lined up to support the president with donations and policy commitments. Their solidarity created the perception that the tech industry had shifted from liberal to conservative.\par \par \uc2 But Mr. Pretti's death helped show some cracks. After that shooting, Mr. Cook, \uc2 Sam Altman\uc2  of \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  and others called on the president to draw down agents.\par \par \uc2 Sundar Pichai\uc2 , \uc2 Google\uc2 's chief executive, hasn't issued a public statement. \uc2 Jeff Dean\uc2 , the chief scientist at \uc2 Google\uc2 's DeepMind A.I. research lab, wrote in a social media post, ''Every person regardless of political affiliation should be denouncing this.''\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2 's immigration petition, which received more than 500 signatures in 24 hours, was organized by No Tech for Apartheid, a group of \uc2 Google\uc2  and \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers who have called for the elimination of the companies' joint cloud computing contract with the Israeli military and government.\par \par \uc2 The petition links to a media report by The Intercept that says \uc2 Google\uc2  is providing cloud services to Customs and Border Protection. It also points to \uc2 Google\uc2 's partnership with \uc2 Palantir\uc2 , the data analysis and technology firm that developed software to track immigrants.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 Google\uc2  spokeswoman said the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  used commercially available cloud infrastructure through \uc2 Google\uc2 's customers, including basic cloud computing and storage services.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Tschiegg said employees didn't know how \uc2 Google\uc2 's technology was being used in those deals and others. The petition asks for a question-and-answer session with management about \uc2 Google\uc2 's contracts with immigration authorities and clarity about whether the company will allow A.I. to be used by the government.\par \par \uc2 ''It seems like they're chasing the money,'' he said. ''It creates a moral and ethical dilemma.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020260207em270003t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
