\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Trump Is Stripping Job Protections From Up to 50,000 Federal Workers\b0\par\par\uc2 By Eileen Sullivan \par \uc2 1,300 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 February 2026\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 18\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The policy change makes it easier for the president to discipline or remove up to 50,000 employees, another push in the administration's campaign to reshape the federal work force.\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration finalized a new policy on Thursday that would strip job protections from up to 50,000 federal workers, a move that would make it easier for President Trump to remove or discipline them, in his latest effort to dismantle the federal work force.\par \par \uc2 Until now, the roughly 4,000 people appointed by the president, known as political appointees, were the only federal workers who could be fired at will. The policy issued on Thursday allows the administration to expand that number to include career employees whom the administration considers to also have policy-related roles. For these employees, any whistle-blower complaints would now be handled inside their agencies rather than by the independent \uc2 Office of Special Counsel\uc2 , as they had previously.\par \par \uc2 The 255-page rule did not say precisely which positions would be affected. The White House will ultimately make that decision, Scott Kupor, the director of the Office of Personnel Management, said on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The announcement represents another push in the administration's campaign to reshape the federal work force, which has included mass firings, layoffs, pressured resignations and early retirements. In total, about 352,000 employees left the federal government in 2025, according to the most recent data from the personnel agency. The new policy is also the latest step the administration has taken to replace nonpartisan civil servants with employees who are ideologically aligned with the president.\par \par \uc2 The proposed version of the rule drew wide criticism, with more than 35,000 submissions over a 45-day public comment period last year. More than 90 percent of the commenters opposed the new policy, according to an analysis by \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  that used artificial intelligence tools to classify each comment's viewpoint.\par \par \uc2 In a statement announcing the final rule, the Office of Personnel Management said that political patronage, loyalty tests and political discrimination in the federal work force were ''explicitly'' prohibited. The rule, which is expected to be published in the Federal Register on Friday, describes the new job category as ''career jobs filled on a nonpartisan basis. Yet they will be at-will positions excepted from adverse action procedures or appeals.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kupor argued that the change helped restore accountability to the federal work force by offering more control of the executive branch to a democratically elected official, the president.\par \par \uc2 But critics question whether the government can be taken at its word after a year of retributive firings, including the dismissal of several whistle-blowers.\par \par \uc2 The rule amounts to ''a huge increase of at-will employment with an administration that has demonstrated a contempt for nonpartisan expertise,'' said \uc2 Max Stier\uc2 , the chief executive of the \uc2 Partnership for Public Service\uc2 , a nonprofit group that promotes an effective federal work force. ''Their track record does not justify trust, and our history as a country demonstrates that these kind of changes lead to worse government results, not better.''\par \par \uc2 The new rule, said Don Kettl, an emeritus professor at the University of Maryland who studies the civil service, ''sweeps away all of the pillars of accountability except responsiveness to the president.''\par \par \uc2 He added, ''That, of course, is patronage under a different name.''\par \par \uc2 Karoline Leavitt, the White House press secretary, called the new rule a ''good thing'' on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 ''I think if people aren't doing their job, they aren't showing up to work, if they're not working hard on behalf of this president, they're not welcome to work for him at all,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump returned to the Oval Office squarely focused on the federal bureaucracy, convinced that it was populated by liberal civil servants who stood in the way of much of his agenda during his first term.\par \par \uc2 ''We need to make it much easier to fire rogue bureaucrats who are deliberately undermining democracy,'' Mr. Trump said in July 2022.\par \par \uc2 The long tradition of a nonpartisan civil service includes a deliberate division between career employees and political appointees. This is in part so career officials with deep expertise can offer context and information to policymakers without fear of getting fired.\par \par \uc2 ''The career staff aren't there to subvert an agenda,'' said Spiro Stefanou, the former administrator of the Agriculture Department's Economic Research Service. ''We're not interested in the agenda. We're interested in your needs. Tell us what you need.''\par \par \uc2 In addition to the rule published on Thursday, Mr. Trump last year changed the federal job application process to include questions about some of his policies, which critics have described as a loyalty test. He established hiring committees to make decisions about who works for the federal government and to screen candidates for partisanship. He stripped union rights from nearly 500,000 employees.\par \par \uc2 The moves have drawn 68 legal challenges as of Thursday, and 61 remain active.\par \par \uc2 The new policy also changes the path for whistle-blowers. Instead of going to an independent agency with a complaint, those federal employees would instead raise the issue with someone ''unbiased'' in the agency's general counsel's office who would investigate, the Office of Personnel Management said on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Tom Devine, the legal director at the Government Accountability Project, a nonprofit group that supports whistle-blower protections, said the new policy turned the Whistleblower Protection Act, a law shielding federal employees from retribution, ''into a bad joke.''\par \par \uc2 His group filed a lawsuit against the administration over an executive order issued last year that called for the new policy. Two unions representing federal employees are also suing the administration in an effort to halt the changes.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump proposed a similar policy toward the end of his first administration, but it never got off the ground. Former President Joseph R. Biden Jr. then issued an order aimed at making it harder for Mr. Trump or a like-minded Republican to revive the initial policy. On his first day back in office, Mr. Trump issued an executive order revoking Mr. Biden's rule and setting up the change announced on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Protections for federal workers date back to the late 1880s, when Congress passed a law requiring the government to hire and fire people based on merit and not political loyalty. Over the years, these protections drew complaints from some administrations about the difficulty of replacing civil servants.\par \par \uc2 ''Because of the maze of rules and regulations with regard to the hiring and firing of federal employees, the executive is more often than not frustrated with its ability to insure a loyal chain of command,'' Fred Malek, an official in former President Richard M. Nixon's administration, wrote in a 1970s guide about federal personnel, including strategies to replace civil servants with politically aligned officials.\par \par \uc2 After the Watergate scandal, Congress passed laws to strengthen civil service protections and improve accountability. The Trump administration has issued policies that appear to chip away at those protections.\par \par \uc2 Soon after he returned to the Oval Office, Mr. Trump fired eight inspectors general and the heads of nine independent agencies and boards. Those fired included the head of the Merit Systems Protection Board and the head of the Office of Special Counsel, in effect cutting off the narrow paths that federal workers had to challenge their firings. And he directed the conversion of thousands of positions in the nonpartisan senior executive service into spots for at-will employees.\par \par \uc2 Emma Schartz contributed reporting from New York, and Dylan Freedman and Erica L. Green from Washington.\par \par \uc2 Emma Schartz contributed reporting from New York, and Dylan Freedman and Erica L. Green from Washington.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020260206em260004i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
