\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Partial Shutdown Will Delay Report On Jobs, Unemployment and Wages\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ben Casselman \par \uc2 400 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 February 2026\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 5\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The report, scheduled for Friday, would have provided data on job growth, unemployment and wages in January.\par \par \uc2 Another government shutdown, another delay in key economic data.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  will not release monthly jobs numbers on Friday as scheduled because of the partial government shutdown, said Emily Liddel, an associate commissioner for the bureau. The report, one of the most closely watched economic indicators each month, would have provided data on job growth, unemployment and wages in January, as well as annual revisions to employment estimates from 2024 and 2025.\par \par \uc2 The report ''will be rescheduled upon the resumption of government funding,'' Ms. Liddel said. A report on job openings and turnover in December, scheduled for release on Tuesday, will also be delayed.\par \par \uc2 The bureau suspended operations over the weekend when funding lapsed for parts of the federal government including its parent agency, the Labor Department.\par \par \uc2 Republican leaders in Congress have expressed confidence that the shutdown will end quickly, perhaps as early as Tuesday. But that would still leave little time for agency employees to finish processing and checking the job data in time for the scheduled Friday morning release -- a process that is a scramble even during normal times.\par \par \uc2 The latest disruption comes as the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  was still struggling to get back on schedule after the monthlong lapse in federal funding last fall. That shutdown led to the cancellation or delay of dozens of economic indicators, including critical data on inflation and the labor market. The agency did not produce an unemployment rate in October for the first time in the 77-year history of the statistic, and distortions related to the pause in data collection are expected to linger for months.\par \par \uc2 Efforts to recover from the shutdown have been hampered by staff attrition, which left positions vacant at all levels of the agency. Even before the lapse in funding, the agency had been forced to cut back data collection in some areas because of insufficient staffing levels.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, President Trump announced that he would appoint Brett Matsumoto, a little-known government economist, to lead the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 . If confirmed by the Senate, Mr. Matsumoto would become the first permanent head of the agency since Mr. Trump fired the previous commissioner, Erika McEntarfer, last August.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020260203em230003c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
