\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 UPS\uc2  Says It Is Cutting Up to 30,000 Jobs to Shore Up Its Profits\b0\par\par\uc2 By Peter Eavis \par \uc2 517 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 January 2026\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 5\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The delivery company said it was planning the cuts this year because it expected to deliver fewer packages for \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , a large but unprofitable customer.\par \par \uc2 UPS\uc2  said on Tuesday that it expected to cut as many as 30,000 jobs this year as part of a continuing effort to reduce costs and shore up profits.\par \par \uc2 Last year, the delivery company eliminated 62,000 positions, by laying off full-time drivers, warehouse workers and managers, as well as seasonal employees. \uc2 UPS\uc2  said on Tuesday that it was planning the additional layoffs this year because it expected to deliver fewer packages for \uc2 Amazon\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The announcement could add to Americans' concerns about the job market. While unemployment has risen only slightly in recent months, hiring has slowed significantly. Consumer confidence plunged this month, partly because a greater share of consumers said jobs were ''hard to get,'' according to the Conference Board.\par \par \uc2 Brian Dykes\uc2 , \uc2 UPS\uc2 's chief financial officer, said this year's job cuts would be in ''operational positions,'' the term \uc2 UPS\uc2  uses for delivery and warehouse workers. He said the reductions would occur ''through attrition'' and by offering ''voluntary separation'' to full-time drivers. \uc2 UPS\uc2  said last year that it wanted to wind down much of its \uc2 Amazon\uc2  business because it was not profitable. \uc2 UPS\uc2  has said \uc2 Amazon\uc2  was its largest customer.\par \par \uc2 UPS\uc2  announced the layoffs on the same day it reported its earnings for 2025. The company had net income of $5.57 billion last year, down nearly 4 percent from $5.78 billion in 2024.\par \par \uc2 UPS\uc2  had around half a million employees at the start of last year, some 300,000 of them members of the \uc2 Teamsters union\uc2 . A \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  spokeswoman did not respond to requests for comment on the layoffs.\par \par \uc2 UPS\uc2 's leaders are restructuring the company to try to bolster profit margins in a difficult time for the delivery business. Last year, the company suffered a sharp drop in packages sent from China after President Trump imposed tariffs on lower-value shipments from the country.\par \par \uc2 Last year, \uc2 UPS\uc2  closed 93 facilities and deployed automation in 57. Mr. Dykes said on Tuesday that the company had identified 24 more buildings for closure in the first half of the year, and that more could follow.\par \par \uc2 Investors appear to be backing \uc2 UPS\uc2 's moves to reinvigorate its business. The company's stock is up over 35 percent from its low last year. Still, over the past year, its shares are down 18 percent, while \uc2 Fedex\uc2 's stock is up 11 percent.\par \par \uc2 On an investor call on Tuesday, \uc2 Carol Tom\u233\'00\'E9\uc2 , \uc2 UPS\uc2 's chief executive, said that ''2025 was a year of considerable progress for \uc2 UPS\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 In November, a \uc2 UPS\uc2  plane crashed in Louisville, Ky., killing three crew members and 12 people on the ground. The plane was a MD-11F jet, made by McDonnell Douglas, which \uc2 Boeing\uc2  acquired in the 1990s. On Tuesday, \uc2 UPS\uc2  said it had retired its MD-11 fleet in the fourth quarter of 2025.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020260128em1s0003x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
