\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 After Pleas From Mamdani and Hochul, Talks Resume in Nurses' Strike\b0\par\par\uc2 By Samantha Latson \par \uc2 895 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 January 2026\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Nearly 15,000 health care workers in New York have been off the job since last week in a labor action affecting some of the city's major hospitals.\par \par \uc2 Responding to pleas from Mayor Zohran Mamdani and Gov. \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  to head back to the bargaining table, the two sides in the New York City nurses' strike resumed negotiations on Thursday in hopes of returning 15,000 workers to their patients' bedside.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Hochul's office said that she has been having discussions with the nurses' union and hospitals every day during the walkout.\par \par \uc2 ''The governor continues to strongly urge both sides to continue negotiations until an agreement is reached that acknowledges the pivotal role nurses play in ensuring our health care systems deliver high-quality care to New Yorkers and their families,'' Nicolette Simmonds, a spokeswoman for Ms. Hochul, said.\par \par \uc2 The president of the \uc2 New York State Nurses Association\uc2 , \uc2 Nancy Hagans\uc2 , said that the union had received a message early this week from Ms. Hochul and Mr. Mamdani asking the union to go back to the bargaining table. The hospitals did not comment about whether they also received a message.\par \par \uc2 ''We made it very clear that we were always willing, available and ready to negotiate and that it was up to them to reach out to the employers,'' Ms. Hagans said in an interview. ''The employers obviously listened to the governor and the mayor.''\par \par \uc2 After negotiating on Thursday, hospital officials said that they are returning to the bargaining table on Friday.\par \par \uc2 ''We have made some progress, but there is still distance to go,'' \uc2 Montefiore Medical Center\uc2  said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 The union said it intends to meet each day with hospital negotiators until a tentative agreement is reached.\par \par \uc2 ''With continued support of mediators, nurses plan on bargaining daily to settle fair contracts that protect patient and nurse safety,'' the union said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Kenneth E. Raske\uc2 , the president of the \uc2 Greater New York Hospital Association\uc2 , a trade group, said that he hopes an agreement will be reached, but he said that negotiations will be a struggle until the union accepts the reality that hospitals are in a financial bind with the passing of President Trump's domestic policy law, known as the ''Big Beautiful Bill.''\par \par \uc2 ''The hard reality is that hospitals are in a tremendous financial squeeze by the bill, and the amount of economic calamity that they're facing is something that we have never seen before,'' Mr. Raske said.\par \par \uc2 The mayor's office did not respond to questions about Mr. Mamdani's role in urging both sides to resume negotiations.\par \par \uc2 The strike, which began on Jan. 12, affects some of the top hospitals in the city, including NewYork-Presbyterian/Columbia, Montefiore and the main campus of Mount Sinai Hospital, along with two other hospitals in the Mount Sinai system.\par \par \uc2 During earlier bargaining sessions, the nurses' union met separately with negotiators from each hospital on different days. Thursday was notable because the union and representatives from the three affected hospital systems met in separate rooms in one building on the same day, starting at 4 p.m.\par \par \uc2 Last week, the three medical systems met with the nurses' union for hours, but failed to reach common ground. After a bargaining session between the nurses and NewYork-Presbyterian/Columbia, the hospital said that the union's proposals were ''unreasonable,'' and that both parties left ''far apart.''\par \par \uc2 Mount Sinai\uc2 's chief executive, Brendan G. Carr, had said that bargaining last Friday had made minimal progress.\par \par \uc2 The strike revolves around a range of issues, including workplace safety, staffing levels and pay and benefits.\par \par \uc2 Nurses have complained for years that many hospital units are persistently understaffed, with nurses caring for too many patients at the same time. They have also sought more protection in dealing with patients who are mentally ill and may become aggressive.\par \par \uc2 Pay is another key matter, although it is rarely mentioned on the nurses' picket lines. Hospital officials have said that nurses are seeking raises that could propel salaries on average past $200,000 a year.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Mamdani backed nurses on the picket line on Tuesday for a second time, alongside Senator Bernie Sanders of Vermont, and called for a resolution to the walkout. Speaking to hundreds of nurses, the mayor said that the strike is not about money, but the worth of nurses in New York City. He said that striking was the last resort and that nurses want to be back at work.\par \par \uc2 ''We call on every side to come back to that negotiating table -- have a swift and urgent resolution,'' Mr. Mamdani said.\par \par \uc2 As nurses continue to walk picket lines in the bitter cold, many expressed concerns about their patients inside. The New York City Emergency Management Department has not received reports of major issues related to the walkout.\par \par \uc2 Glendys Rodriguez, 35, a nurse at NewYork-Presbyterian, said that she intends to stay on strike for as long as it takes union representatives and hospital executives to reach an agreement, although it pains her and her colleagues.\par \par \uc2 ''We had staff crying throughout the week,'' Ms. Rodriguez said. ''We're worried about the patients that couldn't leave the hospital and are there with short staff.''\par 